1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,071
Today we've chosen a title for our talk

2
00:00:02,071 --> 00:00:04,000
namely, "The Flood Returns."

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I wonder what this title brings to your mind?

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,142
Perhaps some of you will think back to

5
00:00:09,142 --> 00:00:12,280
a few years ago, when National Geographic

6
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,123
once produced a well-known documentary

7
00:00:14,123 --> 00:00:15,916
in English it was called Before the Flood

8
00:00:15,916 --> 00:00:20,000
and in Chinese it was given the title "Before the Flood Comes."

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
It, too, was all about paying attention to climate change.

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,019
Now, today is not a movie-appreciation session

11
00:00:27,019 --> 00:00:32,100
but you can actually find that documentary on YouTube if you'd like to watch it.

12
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:35,420
Choosing this title today is really quite fitting.

13
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:39,893
Why is that? Because, as you know, just two or three weeks ago

14
00:00:39,893 --> 00:00:43,620
there was a climate summit held over in the United Kingdom.

15
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:48,220
I imagine none of us went there to join the demonstrations outside.

16
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:50,314
There was also a recent survey

17
00:00:50,314 --> 00:00:53,590
which showed that three-quarters of all young people

18
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:56,920
are absolutely filled with fear about the future.

19
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:59,480
And the reason for that is climate change.

20
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,918
So many young people today

21
00:01:01,918 --> 00:01:09,000
feel utterly lost about what lies ahead, because the road before them seems so uncertain.

22
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,219
The flood is coming.

23
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,895
I remember, back in 2009, there was a news story

24
00:01:16,895 --> 00:01:22,260
about the Indian Ocean island nation of the Maldives.

25
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:26,257
Their cabinet held a meeting, and they deliberately went down to the seabed to do it

26
00:01:26,257 --> 00:01:31,220
that's right, they put on diving gear and held the entire cabinet meeting underwater.

27
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:32,553
Why would they ever do that?

28
00:01:32,553 --> 00:01:37,620
They wanted to draw the attention of the whole world, the attention of the entire international community.

29
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:42,199
Their message was: if we ignore climate change and the sea keeps rising like this

30
00:01:42,199 --> 00:01:45,560
then their whole country will one day end up beneath the sea.

31
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,882
And over here, this is the Solomon Islands

32
00:01:47,882 --> 00:01:52,120
the Solomon Islands, located in the Pacific, in the South Pacific.

33
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:56,920
This clip comes from the current-affairs programme 60 Minutes.

34
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:59,886
The programme interviewed one of the local residents living there.

35
00:01:59,886 --> 00:02:01,531
In the picture at the very top

36
00:02:01,531 --> 00:02:02,816
what you are looking at

37
00:02:02,816 --> 00:02:03,536
is this man

38
00:02:03,536 --> 00:02:05,541
who, back when he was a child

39
00:02:05,541 --> 00:02:05,901
would

40
00:02:05,901 --> 00:02:07,340
often come here to play.

41
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:08,037
But now

42
00:02:08,037 --> 00:02:09,720
this island is completely gone.

43
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:11,940
You can no longer see the island there at all.

44
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:16,040
The sea keeps rising, and a great deal of land has already been swallowed up.

45
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,007
And by the way, over on your

46
00:02:18,007 --> 00:02:19,918
picture in the bottom-right corner

47
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:21,660
is also quite interesting.

48
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:23,036
Because as the sea rises

49
00:02:23,036 --> 00:02:26,500
the plants that once grew there, since seawater is salty

50
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:28,320
those plants begin to die off.

51
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,139
This is a point

52
00:02:29,139 --> 00:02:31,640
that we'll come back to again a little later.

53
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:35,517
As the sea slowly rises, more and more land ends up submerged.

54
00:02:35,517 --> 00:02:38,384
And you'll notice it isn't only the island nations affected.

55
00:02:38,384 --> 00:02:40,860
This here is Venice, in Italy.

56
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:42,570
Because the sea level keeps rising

57
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:44,974
in recent years it has again and again

58
00:02:44,974 --> 00:02:46,500
suffered from flooding.

59
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,367
I wonder if you've ever heard of the so-called "MOSE Project"?

60
00:02:49,367 --> 00:02:53,400
They planned to build a whole set of floodgates to hold back the seawater.

61
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:56,480
This was the flooding as it looked back in 2019.

62
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,780
That bridge in the background is really quite famous.

63
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:00,823
If you've ever been to the Venetian hotel

64
00:03:00,823 --> 00:03:03,640
there's a bridge just like it outside, isn't there?

65
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,780
This spot here was originally a canal.

66
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:08,451
But the seawater came flooding in, all the way up to

67
00:03:08,451 --> 00:03:12,340
the shops and the pavements, and you can see the boats sitting right here.

68
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:16,297
And it wasn't only 2019, it flooded all over again in 2020.

69
00:03:16,297 --> 00:03:18,842
In other words, in recent years it floods over and over.

70
00:03:18,842 --> 00:03:33,680
Here in New Zealand, I don't know if you've followed this, but up above you there's a country called Indonesia, and Indonesia's capital is Jakarta, which recently announced it's moving the capital. Why is that?

71
00:03:33,680 --> 00:04:03,680
Because Jakarta sits right by the sea, and as the waters rise it floods constantly; every time it rains, every time a storm blows in, it floods. The reason is that so many people there pump up groundwater, which has caused Jakarta's land to subside and keep sinking; add to that the rising sea, and every year the flooding grows worse, so they've decided to relocate to the island of Kalimantan.

72
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:07,802
This is actually affecting many countries and a great many people.

73
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:10,658
For example, over in Bangladesh

74
00:04:10,658 --> 00:04:12,807
many people along the coast have already moved away

75
00:04:12,807 --> 00:04:15,200
to areas further north and further inland.

76
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:16,662
These people are what we call

77
00:04:16,662 --> 00:04:18,280
climate refugees.

78
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:21,299
And it's all because the sea keeps on rising.

79
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:23,879
But it isn't only the rising of the sea.

80
00:04:23,879 --> 00:04:27,271
This scene here, if you follow the news, you'll surely recognise.

81
00:04:27,271 --> 00:04:29,182
This July, in Henan, in China

82
00:04:29,182 --> 00:04:31,142
because of relentless torrential rain

83
00:04:31,142 --> 00:04:36,320
you saw scenes exactly like this: the tunnels flooded, and the casualties were severe.

84
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:41,196
The picture in the lower left is the subway line that was flooded that very day

85
00:04:41,196 --> 00:04:46,479
and inside the subway carriages, the water rose right up to people's chests.

86
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:51,820
So why does so much rain fall, and why does it flood this badly?

87
00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:55,261
It's because, as the climate warms and the air heats up

88
00:04:55,261 --> 00:04:58,856
the amount of moisture the air can hold becomes much greater

89
00:04:58,856 --> 00:05:03,221
and so these bouts of torrential rain, these sudden cloudbursts

90
00:05:03,221 --> 00:05:06,200
become extremely severe the moment they hit.

91
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:09,683
In just a few hours you can get several days' worth of rain

92
00:05:09,683 --> 00:05:12,934
and in just a few days, a whole year's worth of rain.

93
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:15,680
Do you recognise where this place is?

94
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:16,700
Probably not, I'd guess.

95
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:18,674
This, too, was in the month of July

96
00:05:18,674 --> 00:05:20,705
but this one is over in New Zealand.

97
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,635
It rained, and after just a single weekend

98
00:05:23,635 --> 00:05:26,680
this was the scene once the rain had finally stopped.

99
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:28,096
So it isn't only China

100
00:05:28,096 --> 00:05:30,078
and it isn't only the Northern Hemisphere

101
00:05:30,078 --> 00:05:32,580
the Southern Hemisphere is exactly the same.

102
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:33,706
Just a few days ago

103
00:05:33,706 --> 00:05:38,209
if you follow the news, you'll know Vancouver was hit by severe rainfall

104
00:05:38,209 --> 00:05:42,820
which cut off all the transport links between Vancouver and the surrounding areas.

105
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:45,260
Because so many of the highways were flooded

106
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:46,313
and many of the bridges

107
00:05:46,313 --> 00:05:47,940
were simply washed away.

108
00:05:47,940 --> 00:05:49,403
If you look over to the right

109
00:05:49,403 --> 00:05:51,493
that there is the railway track

110
00:05:51,493 --> 00:05:52,486
the railway line

111
00:05:52,486 --> 00:05:55,360
and it, too, was destroyed.

112
00:05:55,360 --> 00:06:00,004
And you'll find it isn't only cloudbursts that cause this kind of damage.

113
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:07,360
In recent years, if you pay attention, every time a storm comes you'll hear this term, the "storm surge."

114
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:11,064
A storm surge means this: at the time when a storm blows in

115
00:06:11,064 --> 00:06:13,434
the eye of the storm is an area of low pressure

116
00:06:13,434 --> 00:06:15,360
which pulls the seawater upward.

117
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:19,360
So every time a storm hits, the tide rises higher and higher.

118
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:21,360
And that is what we call a storm surge.

119
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:25,365
This was back in 2005, over in the United States

120
00:06:25,365 --> 00:06:29,951
the scene after the rising sea came and struck New Orleans.

121
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:31,821
So it really doesn't matter what your

122
00:06:31,821 --> 00:06:33,679
particular city happens to be like

123
00:06:33,679 --> 00:06:35,854
even a country like the United States

124
00:06:35,854 --> 00:06:37,440
you'll find it floods just the same.

125
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,297
As I mentioned just a moment ago

126
00:06:39,297 --> 00:06:43,320
rising seawater causes the plants along the coast to die.

127
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:49,720
In fact, people later studied why Katrina caused such catastrophic flooding in New Orleans.

128
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:52,564
On the one hand, of course, it was Katrina itself

129
00:06:52,564 --> 00:06:54,270
the winds were especially strong

130
00:06:54,270 --> 00:06:56,640
and on top of that, some of the levees broke.

131
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:00,625
But there was also one very important reason, and it is this:

132
00:07:00,625 --> 00:07:02,023
as the sea kept on rising

133
00:07:02,023 --> 00:07:05,231
it killed off the plants in many of the coastal marshlands

134
00:07:05,231 --> 00:07:06,784
so that along the coastline

135
00:07:06,784 --> 00:07:10,200
there was no longer any buffer zone left to protect it.

136
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:12,415
So when the storm finally came

137
00:07:12,415 --> 00:07:15,184
the winds, and the great sea waves

138
00:07:15,184 --> 00:07:17,620
struck the city directly.

139
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:18,387
This here was

140
00:07:18,387 --> 00:07:21,335
the situation with Katrina back in 2005.

141
00:07:21,335 --> 00:07:24,934
And this is Hurricane Sandy, in 2012.

142
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:28,987
I don't know if you recognise where this is, but this is New York.

143
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:31,700
right on the east coast of the United States.

144
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:37,515
And it's worth pointing out that New York actually lies at over 40 degrees north latitude.

145
00:07:37,515 --> 00:07:39,817
The word "hurricane" simply means a great wind.

146
00:07:39,817 --> 00:07:42,684
If it were in Hong Kong, we would call it a "typhoon."

147
00:07:42,684 --> 00:07:46,040
Its proper scientific name is actually the "tropical cyclone."

148
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:48,860
that is to say, a cyclone of the tropics.

149
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:53,860
As the name itself suggests, these storms all form in the tropical regions.

150
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:56,860
Yet New York sits at over forty degrees north.

151
00:07:56,860 --> 00:08:02,004
So you can see how global warming is now driving many tropical disasters

152
00:08:02,004 --> 00:08:07,860
such as storms striking far to the north, and it seems to be becoming a new normal.

153
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:11,860
Likewise, if you've ever been to New York, you'll know the lay of the land there.

154
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:14,646
The rising sea, together with the storm surge

155
00:08:14,646 --> 00:08:18,187
flooded Manhattan, specifically Lower Manhattan.

156
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,416
You know, New York actually has a nickname

157
00:08:22,416 --> 00:08:24,680
the "City that Never Sleeps."

158
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:30,276
Lower Manhattan is basically a financial centre

159
00:08:30,276 --> 00:08:33,661
lit up around the clock, twenty-four hours a day

160
00:08:33,661 --> 00:08:34,806
because of all of the

161
00:08:34,806 --> 00:08:36,100
financial trading.

162
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:40,098
As it turns out, many financial institutions keep their computers down underground.

163
00:08:40,098 --> 00:08:41,159
And that particular flood

164
00:08:41,159 --> 00:08:42,260
caused very serious damage.

165
00:08:42,260 --> 00:08:46,620
In fact, it took several years afterward to slowly recover from it.

166
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:49,174
The subways were flooded in exactly the same way

167
00:08:49,174 --> 00:08:51,206
and swarms of rats came scurrying out

168
00:08:51,206 --> 00:08:55,153
as if giving the whole city a thorough washing.

169
00:08:56,340 --> 00:08:57,711
In this picture on your right

170
00:08:57,711 --> 00:09:02,740
it's hard to imagine a subway station, usually so busy with people, completely underwater.

171
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:05,560
You can see the divers there carrying out the search and rescue.

172
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:10,668
This is Macau, back in 2017, struck by the typhoon Hato.

173
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:15,754
Because of this storm surge, the seawater came pouring back inland

174
00:09:15,754 --> 00:09:17,840
leaving the whole city in this state.

175
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:20,061
And here, too, the casualties were severe.

176
00:09:20,061 --> 00:09:23,709
I didn't actually put up any of the photos from Hong Kong

177
00:09:23,709 --> 00:09:25,367
but you'll notice that every single year

178
00:09:25,367 --> 00:09:26,238
whenever a storm comes

179
00:09:26,238 --> 00:09:30,673
in recent years, Hato in 2017, and Mangkhut in 2018

180
00:09:30,673 --> 00:09:33,617
you'll find the coastal areas, whether it's Tseung Kwan O

181
00:09:33,617 --> 00:09:36,394
or Heng Fa Chuen and other low-lying spots

182
00:09:36,394 --> 00:09:38,840
the coastline gets battered by the waves again and again.

183
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:39,922
And speaking of all this

184
00:09:39,922 --> 00:09:43,120
I'm reminded of a verse in the Bible, in Luke chapter 21.

185
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:47,300
The Bible describes the state of the world before the Lord returns

186
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:50,144
and among those words is this one line:

187
00:09:50,144 --> 00:09:52,060
on the earth distress of nations

188
00:09:52,060 --> 00:09:57,110
in perplexity because of the roaring of the sea and the waves.

189
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:01,545
The Bible also speaks about our last days

190
00:10:01,545 --> 00:10:06,421
and one of the disasters it names seems to be the very waves of the sea.

191
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:16,715
We've just been looking at this rising of the sea, at storm surges; and in fact, I first noticed this verse back in 2004

192
00:10:16,715 --> 00:10:20,440
and that was because of the great South Asian tsunami.

193
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:25,811
And of course there was also the tsunami triggered by the great East Japan earthquake in 2011.

194
00:10:25,811 --> 00:10:27,929
These events all seem to be telling us something

195
00:10:27,929 --> 00:10:30,862
this very situation, that because of the waves of the sea

196
00:10:30,862 --> 00:10:32,980
people are gripped with fear and confusion.

197
00:10:32,980 --> 00:10:37,624
And this reminds me of something the Bible records in particular.

198
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:42,980
When the Lord Jesus was on earth, He once helped His disciples out on the Sea of Galilee.

199
00:10:42,980 --> 00:10:49,320
They suddenly ran into a fierce storm; now, some of the disciples were fishermen and had seen storms before

200
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:53,197
but the storm was so violent that they even thought they were about to die.

201
00:10:53,197 --> 00:10:58,980
Yet the Lord was there asleep in the stern; they woke Him, crying out, "Lord, we're perishing!"

202
00:10:58,980 --> 00:11:04,100
The Lord got up, rebuked the wind and the waves, and made the whole storm completely calm.

203
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:07,180
I find this account recorded in the Bible deeply meaningful.

204
00:11:07,180 --> 00:11:09,154
On the one hand, you see the Lord Jesus

205
00:11:09,154 --> 00:11:12,288
He truly is able to rescue us out of every danger.

206
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:16,900
And on the other, it shows that the Lord can calm any storm.

207
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:19,107
No matter what it is, in your own life

208
00:11:19,107 --> 00:11:20,608
whatever hardship you happen to meet

209
00:11:20,608 --> 00:11:23,300
whatever blows come, whatever storms arise

210
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:25,860
the Lord is able to calm every single storm.

211
00:11:25,860 --> 00:11:29,633
If a Christian friend has invited you here today

212
00:11:29,633 --> 00:11:39,386
the truth is, your Christian friends would be very glad to share with you how the Lord has helped them calm the storms in their own lives.

213
00:11:42,860 --> 00:11:45,000
Alright, let me pose a question to everyone.

214
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:51,860
In this glass here, it's filled up with ice, and filled with water too, right up to the very brim.

215
00:11:51,860 --> 00:12:00,860
After a while, once the ice has melted, here is my question for you all: will the water overflow the glass, or will it not?

216
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:01,615
It won't.

217
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:05,530
It won't, will it? Very impressive indeed.

218
00:12:06,860 --> 00:12:20,860
So what am I really getting at? Water is a very special thing, and not just in its structure, because it is H₂O; the H is hydrogen, which, as you know, is itself flammable, and the O is oxygen, which itself supports combustion.

219
00:12:20,860 --> 00:12:22,193
And yet, remarkably

220
00:12:22,193 --> 00:12:26,917
hydrogen combined together with oxygen becomes water, and water is able to put out fire.

221
00:12:26,917 --> 00:12:31,359
There's another special thing too: everything expands with heat and contracts with cold.

222
00:12:31,359 --> 00:12:35,200
The lower the temperature falls, the more the volume of water shrinks down.

223
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:36,883
But here is the strange part.

224
00:12:36,980 --> 00:12:38,957
When it reaches 4 degrees Celsius

225
00:12:38,957 --> 00:12:39,685
its volume

226
00:12:39,685 --> 00:12:42,027
shrinks right down to its smallest point

227
00:12:42,027 --> 00:12:44,420
and then it shrinks no further at all.

228
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:49,780
At 3 degrees, then 2, then 1, and at 0 degrees when it freezes, the volume actually starts to expand.

229
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:53,727
And so its density, instead of rising, decreases.

230
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:58,480
And because the density is now lower, you'll notice the ice floats on top of the water.

231
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:01,145
So you see, ice really is quite a special thing.

232
00:13:01,145 --> 00:13:04,048
And why is that? I believe it is the mercy of God.

233
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:09,403
Just think about it, if water became denser as it froze and sank down to the bottom

234
00:13:09,403 --> 00:13:12,593
then every single living creature in the sea would die.

235
00:13:12,593 --> 00:13:18,700
Just imagine it, it would be like a person walking to the edge of a cliff, and the moment they fall off, they're dead.

236
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:27,700
But you'll find it's a wonderful thing: when water freezes, its volume actually expands instead, its density drops, and so it floats on the water.

237
00:13:27,700 --> 00:13:32,707
There's one more special thing too. What is it? You can see the ice looks quite large, can't you?

238
00:13:32,707 --> 00:13:44,700
But when the ice turns back into water, its volume actually shrinks; because, as I said, freezing makes it bigger, and turning back to water makes it smaller again.

239
00:13:44,700 --> 00:13:51,318
And the volume it loses is precisely the part that had been sticking out above the water.

240
00:13:51,620 --> 00:13:55,214
So some people say, when you see an iceberg, you'd better get well away from it.

241
00:13:55,214 --> 00:13:59,656
In fact, the part of an iceberg sticking out above the surface is only about 11 percent.

242
00:13:59,656 --> 00:14:02,100
The other 89 percent lies below the water.

243
00:14:02,100 --> 00:14:06,460
So a full ninety percent of any iceberg lies down beneath the surface.

244
00:14:06,460 --> 00:14:08,396
So when you spot an iceberg from far away

245
00:14:08,396 --> 00:14:12,100
the ice beneath the water may already be very close to you, without your ever knowing it.

246
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:32,069
Now, ice that floats on the ocean, up on the surface of the water, doesn't actually cause the sea to rise; it only leaves the polar bears with nowhere to stand, because as the ice melts they may have to travel very far to find a foothold.

247
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:35,757
In other words, what really causes the sea to rise

248
00:14:35,757 --> 00:14:38,253
is mainly the ice sitting on the land.

249
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:41,640
And what do people usually mean by that?

250
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:43,962
They mean Greenland, and Antarctica.

251
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:47,040
Here's a news story for you.

252
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:48,502
Just this past August

253
00:14:48,502 --> 00:14:50,061
you'd find it quite remarkable

254
00:14:50,061 --> 00:14:53,180
it rained in Greenland for the very first time.

255
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:55,563
At first I really didn't think much of it.

256
00:14:55,563 --> 00:14:57,766
My immediate reaction was something like this:

257
00:14:57,766 --> 00:15:00,960
surely it must have rained in Greenland before?

258
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:03,572
Why do you think this made it into the headlines?

259
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:09,960
Well, just think about it: Greenland is to the east of Canada, very far to the north.

260
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:14,720
It really shouldn't be raining there at all, it should be snowing, do you see?

261
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:20,960
So the first recorded rainfall in Greenland shows that the temperature has genuinely risen.

262
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:25,082
And on your right, you can see those dogs pulling along a sled

263
00:15:25,082 --> 00:15:29,319
they're not walking on water; it's that the surface layer of the ice

264
00:15:29,319 --> 00:15:31,931
is melting very, very badly.

265
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:33,875
So when you photograph it from this angle

266
00:15:33,875 --> 00:15:35,248
it looks as though the dogs

267
00:15:35,248 --> 00:15:37,712
are walking right across the surface of the water.

268
00:15:37,712 --> 00:15:39,273
This is actually in Greenland

269
00:15:39,273 --> 00:15:40,886
that is to say, Greenland

270
00:15:40,886 --> 00:15:43,280
the melting of the surface ice there.

271
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:47,150
Someone has calculated that if all of Greenland's ice were to melt

272
00:15:47,150 --> 00:15:52,240
the sea would rise by around twenty-odd feet, roughly two storeys high.

273
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:52,916
Two storeys

274
00:15:52,916 --> 00:15:58,120
twenty-odd feet is really very high; I wonder how close to the sea your homes happen to be.

275
00:15:58,120 --> 00:15:59,170
I wonder for all of you

276
00:15:59,170 --> 00:16:00,503
which floor you live on.

277
00:16:00,503 --> 00:16:05,560
But what if the water, the ice of Antarctica, were to melt, what then?

278
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:09,360
Antarctica holds nine times as much ice as Greenland does.

279
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:15,223
That means if the ice of Antarctica and the ice of Greenland were to melt together

280
00:16:15,223 --> 00:16:20,448
the world's sea level would rise up by around 21 or 22 storeys.

281
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:24,140
Would you like to picture that scene for a moment?

282
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:28,049
And this is only the rise caused by the land ice melting.

283
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:35,369
In fact, when the temperature rises, there is another factor, another influence, that is very significant.

284
00:16:35,369 --> 00:16:39,529
It's that, as the temperature climbs, the seawater itself expands.

285
00:16:39,529 --> 00:16:45,160
And this expansion of the sea, which raises the sea level, is actually a very serious matter.

286
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:47,830
This February there was a news story.

287
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:53,060
For the very first time, a Russian icebreaker was able to cross

288
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:58,540
a passage in the Arctic Ocean that had never once been navigable before.

289
00:16:58,540 --> 00:16:59,832
So what does this actually tell us?

290
00:16:59,832 --> 00:17:02,874
It tells us that in the Arctic Ocean, at the North Pole

291
00:17:02,874 --> 00:17:05,440
the ice has grown very much thinner.

292
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:06,477
Now you might well say

293
00:17:06,477 --> 00:17:08,975
hold on a moment, you've only just finished telling us

294
00:17:08,975 --> 00:17:10,813
you said that at the North Pole

295
00:17:10,813 --> 00:17:14,160
ice melting on the sea won't affect the height of the water.

296
00:17:14,160 --> 00:17:15,089
But here's the thing

297
00:17:15,089 --> 00:17:16,714
there are still other effects.

298
00:17:17,480 --> 00:17:20,800
In recent years, haven't you often been hearing about heatwaves?

299
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:23,064
We went through one this June.

300
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:28,280
This June a heatwave struck Canada, and Canada is very far to the north.

301
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:31,360
Vancouver sits at 50 degrees north latitude.

302
00:17:31,360 --> 00:17:32,927
But just think of Vancouver's

303
00:17:32,927 --> 00:17:35,482
neighbour, the city of Calgary.

304
00:17:35,700 --> 00:17:36,745
In Calgary

305
00:17:36,745 --> 00:17:39,299
it reached over 40 degrees Celsius.

306
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:41,840
You might well find that very strange.

307
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:46,322
Why would there be a heatwave so far north? If you look at the heatwaves of recent years

308
00:17:46,322 --> 00:17:50,118
they're often caused by one thing, what's called a "heat dome."

309
00:17:50,118 --> 00:17:52,840
In Chinese it's popularly known as a "heat lid."

310
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:54,617
a lid, as in a covering lid.

311
00:17:54,617 --> 00:17:57,720
Just imagine you're cooking something, boiling some water.

312
00:17:57,720 --> 00:17:59,804
With a lid on, or without a lid

313
00:17:59,804 --> 00:18:00,780
isn't it different?

314
00:18:00,780 --> 00:18:03,688
With the lid on, doesn't it come to a boil much faster?

315
00:18:03,688 --> 00:18:05,666
Because all of that steam simply can't escape.

316
00:18:05,666 --> 00:18:06,555
The steam can't get out

317
00:18:06,555 --> 00:18:07,887
so it boils very quickly.

318
00:18:07,887 --> 00:18:09,503
It's exactly the same principle at work.

319
00:18:09,503 --> 00:18:10,842
Because in this picture, you can see

320
00:18:10,842 --> 00:18:13,073
can you see, there's a jet stream right here

321
00:18:13,073 --> 00:18:16,400
that is, a high-speed air current, way up high in the atmosphere.

322
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:17,773
It forms an area of high pressure

323
00:18:17,773 --> 00:18:20,156
so that all the air trapped down beneath it

324
00:18:20,156 --> 00:18:22,336
even though it isn't hot in itself

325
00:18:22,336 --> 00:18:23,386
simply cannot disperse.

326
00:18:23,386 --> 00:18:24,880
It has no way at all to escape.

327
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:26,455
It's just like putting a lid on the pot.

328
00:18:26,455 --> 00:18:28,272
So that within a very short span of time

329
00:18:28,272 --> 00:18:29,322
all of that trapped air

330
00:18:29,322 --> 00:18:30,695
heats up extremely fast.

331
00:18:30,695 --> 00:18:33,078
So just think about it, at over 50 degrees north

332
00:18:33,078 --> 00:18:35,461
you can still record over 40 degrees Celsius.

333
00:18:35,461 --> 00:18:37,035
That really is quite staggering.

334
00:18:37,035 --> 00:18:37,439
Alright.

335
00:18:37,439 --> 00:18:39,095
But there's something I want you to notice.

336
00:18:39,095 --> 00:18:41,276
I'm not really here just to introduce the heat dome.

337
00:18:41,276 --> 00:18:43,376
Let me add one more term while we're at it.

338
00:18:43,376 --> 00:18:44,950
This kind of air-current phenomenon

339
00:18:44,950 --> 00:18:46,889
is actually called an "Omega Block."

340
00:18:46,889 --> 00:18:51,400
because its shape closely resembles the Greek letter Omega.

341
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:56,508
What I really want you to notice is the influence of the high-altitude jet stream.

342
00:18:57,400 --> 00:18:59,954
You'll find that because of the way it wanders

343
00:18:59,954 --> 00:19:03,902
hot air gets trapped, and so you get heat domes, and heatwaves.

344
00:19:04,400 --> 00:19:07,262
And likewise, because of this very same wandering

345
00:19:07,262 --> 00:19:10,068
you'll see it drives the cold air out of the Arctic

346
00:19:10,068 --> 00:19:14,221
sending those polar vortexes down toward the south

347
00:19:14,221 --> 00:19:17,700
reaching much further south into some regions.

348
00:19:17,700 --> 00:19:21,197
So in recent years you constantly hear news of

349
00:19:21,197 --> 00:19:23,899
many places suffering from heatwaves

350
00:19:23,899 --> 00:19:25,329
while at the very same time

351
00:19:25,329 --> 00:19:26,389
many other places

352
00:19:26,389 --> 00:19:28,720
are hitting record-breaking cold.

353
00:19:28,720 --> 00:19:29,913
There was even a time

354
00:19:29,913 --> 00:19:31,262
on one occasion

355
00:19:31,262 --> 00:19:32,040
when Toronto

356
00:19:32,040 --> 00:19:35,360
recorded a ground temperature colder than the Arctic itself.

357
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:36,640
And why is that?

358
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:38,960
It's because of this very phenomenon.

359
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:40,575
You might want to ask a question here.

360
00:19:40,575 --> 00:19:43,820
Why does this jet stream wander about like this?

361
00:19:43,820 --> 00:19:46,084
Well, the reason the jet stream wanders

362
00:19:46,084 --> 00:19:48,696
is that it has grown weaker.

363
00:19:49,460 --> 00:19:52,520
Because it has weakened, because it has slowed right down.

364
00:19:52,520 --> 00:19:54,320
Just imagine it, it's like a river.

365
00:19:54,320 --> 00:19:57,268
when it reaches its lower course, when it reaches the flat plains

366
00:19:57,268 --> 00:20:00,175
doesn't it slow right down? Because the slope is no longer as steep.

367
00:20:00,175 --> 00:20:03,123
And in order to keep moving forward, doesn't it begin to wind about?

368
00:20:03,123 --> 00:20:04,981
That river starts to meander, doesn't it?

369
00:20:04,981 --> 00:20:07,040
Well, it's exactly the same situation here.

370
00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:08,260
Because the jet stream

371
00:20:08,260 --> 00:20:09,943
has weakened and slowed down

372
00:20:09,943 --> 00:20:13,542
it begins to wander; and because of this wandering

373
00:20:13,542 --> 00:20:21,379
the heatwaves I spoke of earlier, and those invasions of Arctic cold, are becoming more and more frequent.

374
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:26,528
Alright, so now you'll ask a question: why has it grown weaker?

375
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Well, this actually proves one thing: that the Arctic has warmed a great deal.

376
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:35,957
Because the difference between the Arctic's temperature and that of the surrounding regions

377
00:20:35,957 --> 00:20:42,055
the greater that difference is, the faster the surrounding air rushes in toward the Arctic, and the stronger the jet stream becomes.

378
00:20:42,055 --> 00:20:49,253
But once the Arctic warms up, that temperature gap shrinks, and so this jet stream grows weaker.

379
00:20:52,000 --> 00:21:02,000
In fact, research shows that global temperature change isn't uniform at all, and in the Arctic the temperature is rising four times faster than in the surrounding regions.

380
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:05,069
In other words, the Arctic region is warming up far more quickly.

381
00:21:05,069 --> 00:21:10,000
You might want to ask a question, namely: why is it warming so very fast?

382
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:14,100
One of the main reasons is the great Siberian wildfires.

383
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:19,683
This fire has actually been burning for several years now; and in fact, the largest fire of the entire 21st century

384
00:21:19,683 --> 00:21:23,320
is the great blaze burning this year in Siberia, in Russia.

385
00:21:23,320 --> 00:21:27,666
The area of this one fire, compared with North America's

386
00:21:27,666 --> 00:21:30,350
the wildfires of California and Canada put together

387
00:21:30,350 --> 00:21:35,080
add to that the fires of Europe, and of Turkey, and this one is still larger in area.

388
00:21:35,080 --> 00:21:36,976
This was captured from high up above

389
00:21:36,976 --> 00:21:43,280
the situation of the Siberian fires; and you'll notice, aren't there many spots where there's no smoke at all?

390
00:21:43,280 --> 00:21:47,380
Why is it so strange? Why can I see only smoke, and no actual flames?

391
00:21:47,380 --> 00:21:53,020
It turns out the fires in Siberia, within the Arctic Circle, are very peculiar indeed.

392
00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:58,380
This kind of fire has a name: it's called a "zombie fire." And as the name suggests, do you know what it means?

393
00:21:58,380 --> 00:22:01,200
You can't smother it, and you can't put it out.

394
00:22:01,200 --> 00:22:04,929
Why is that? Because inside the Arctic Circle it's so cold

395
00:22:04,929 --> 00:22:06,719
that after many of the plants die

396
00:22:06,719 --> 00:22:08,360
they don't decompose.

397
00:22:08,360 --> 00:22:11,700
So beneath the soil there's a great deal of organic matter.

398
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:14,260
And when a fire breaks out, sometimes these flames

399
00:22:14,260 --> 00:22:16,300
will burn their way down underground.

400
00:22:16,300 --> 00:22:18,106
And once they've burned down underground

401
00:22:18,106 --> 00:22:18,678
you'll find that

402
00:22:18,678 --> 00:22:20,660
you simply cannot put them out.

403
00:22:20,660 --> 00:22:23,333
But given the chance, they'll burn their way back up again

404
00:22:23,333 --> 00:22:24,500
to the surface.

405
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:27,560
So this fire has, in fact, been burning for several years now.

406
00:22:27,560 --> 00:22:29,095
Does it have any consequences?

407
00:22:29,095 --> 00:22:32,926
This here is the ground, captured in satellite imagery.

408
00:22:32,940 --> 00:22:35,040
You can see all those points of fire, can't you?

409
00:22:35,040 --> 00:22:37,340
Underground, it's still burning away.

410
00:22:37,340 --> 00:22:40,126
But the moment it gets a chance, it breaks right out to the surface.

411
00:22:40,126 --> 00:22:44,000
The whole landscape smoking over, that's exactly what's happening here.

412
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,060
You might ask, the great Siberian fires

413
00:22:46,060 --> 00:22:48,886
these Arctic Circle fires, what impact do they really have?

414
00:22:48,886 --> 00:22:51,188
Last year there was a news story just like this.

415
00:22:51,188 --> 00:22:55,520
You see the picture at the top, that river, has it not turned completely red?

416
00:22:55,520 --> 00:22:57,320
And it's not because of any blood.

417
00:22:57,820 --> 00:22:58,807
It's rather because of

418
00:22:58,807 --> 00:22:59,736
an oil spill.

419
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:02,633
You see, the ground in the Arctic Circle

420
00:23:02,633 --> 00:23:07,680
is called permafrost, ground that has built up a great deal of ice and frozen solid.

421
00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:11,759
And on top of it sit many oil refineries, and many storage tanks.

422
00:23:11,759 --> 00:23:15,486
But as the Arctic Circle warms and this ice melts away

423
00:23:15,486 --> 00:23:21,680
many of these facilities, the storage tanks for instance, subside, and start leaking oil.

424
00:23:21,680 --> 00:23:27,775
This was the spill back in May of last year, and it was a genuine ecological disaster.

425
00:23:28,680 --> 00:23:33,963
If you go to Siberia these days, you'll often see craters just like these.

426
00:23:34,320 --> 00:23:39,680
Looking at the people standing beside them, you can grasp just how huge these craters are.

427
00:23:39,680 --> 00:23:42,000
So why are there such enormous craters?

428
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:46,340
Who would have the time to dig such holes? In fact, they weren't dug out at all.

429
00:23:46,340 --> 00:23:49,823
It's because the permafrost has warmed up and the ice has melted

430
00:23:49,823 --> 00:23:54,235
releasing the methane that had been trapped down in the ground.

431
00:23:57,360 --> 00:23:58,637
And this methane

432
00:23:58,637 --> 00:23:59,450
here you can see

433
00:23:59,450 --> 00:24:01,423
it's rather like a bubble.

434
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:03,860
That is, down beneath the soil

435
00:24:03,860 --> 00:24:05,610
beneath all that vegetation

436
00:24:05,610 --> 00:24:07,560
there's a pocket of methane gas.

437
00:24:07,560 --> 00:24:09,703
And sometimes it simply explodes

438
00:24:09,703 --> 00:24:12,560
which created those very craters you saw a moment ago.

439
00:24:12,560 --> 00:24:16,160
And those bubbles down beneath the water's surface

440
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:18,160
are all of them methane.

441
00:24:18,160 --> 00:24:19,993
If you go and find some of the photos online

442
00:24:19,993 --> 00:24:21,285
there are people playing around with it

443
00:24:21,285 --> 00:24:22,285
right there on the ice

444
00:24:22,285 --> 00:24:25,160
they bore a hole into it, and then set it alight.

445
00:24:25,160 --> 00:24:26,321
Now this methane

446
00:24:26,321 --> 00:24:27,772
as a greenhouse gas

447
00:24:27,772 --> 00:24:32,881
methane's effect is actually 20 times greater than that of carbon dioxide.

448
00:24:35,300 --> 00:24:39,623
In other words, take a given volume of carbon dioxide and its effect on temperature

449
00:24:39,623 --> 00:24:43,480
swap it out for methane, and its effect is a full twenty times as strong.

450
00:24:43,480 --> 00:24:45,136
So someone has actually studied this

451
00:24:45,136 --> 00:24:46,226
and what did he say?

452
00:24:46,226 --> 00:24:49,709
He said that once Siberia's methane is released

453
00:24:49,709 --> 00:24:55,456
the impact would equal that of all the world's carbon dioxide emissions combined.

454
00:24:57,360 --> 00:25:00,204
That is an enormous crisis indeed.

455
00:25:00,520 --> 00:25:02,871
This also brings to my mind, in the Bible

456
00:25:02,871 --> 00:25:04,020
another verse.

457
00:25:04,020 --> 00:25:08,328
In the Book of Revelation, speaking of the days of disaster still to come

458
00:25:08,328 --> 00:25:10,310
an angel pours out his bowl upon the sun

459
00:25:10,310 --> 00:25:13,240
and the sun is given power to scorch people with fire.

460
00:25:13,240 --> 00:25:18,431
Already, some places are hitting over 40 degrees; I mentioned Vancouver just now

461
00:25:18,431 --> 00:25:19,640
and Calgary.

462
00:25:19,640 --> 00:25:23,181
And some places have even reached over 50 degrees.

463
00:25:23,980 --> 00:25:27,463
As I was saying, if this methane were to be released in that way

464
00:25:27,463 --> 00:25:30,598
then for the temperature of the whole planet

465
00:25:30,598 --> 00:25:32,804
the effect could come on very quickly

466
00:25:32,804 --> 00:25:34,313
very abruptly indeed.

467
00:25:36,060 --> 00:25:37,660
So if you were to ask me

468
00:25:37,660 --> 00:25:40,425
this idea of the sun scorching people

469
00:25:40,425 --> 00:25:41,298
I don't think it is

470
00:25:41,298 --> 00:25:43,480
some far-fetched fairy tale at all.

471
00:25:43,480 --> 00:25:46,673
In fact, there are already signs now showing us

472
00:25:46,673 --> 00:25:48,182
that this global warming

473
00:25:48,182 --> 00:25:48,937
can indeed

474
00:25:48,937 --> 00:25:50,156
turn very fast

475
00:25:50,156 --> 00:25:51,897
and grow very severe.

476
00:25:53,480 --> 00:25:55,330
In this picture here, it lets us see

477
00:25:55,330 --> 00:25:57,780
the situation of the great forest fires.

478
00:25:57,780 --> 00:26:00,780
We've already talked about the Siberian blaze.

479
00:26:00,780 --> 00:26:01,830
You can see it right here.

480
00:26:01,830 --> 00:26:03,780
And here you can also see several more fires.

481
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:06,218
This one here is the Amazon rainforest.

482
00:26:06,780 --> 00:26:09,780
This one is the Congo Basin, in Africa.

483
00:26:09,780 --> 00:26:12,041
And this one is Borneo, in Indonesia.

484
00:26:12,041 --> 00:26:13,340
All of them are tropical regions

485
00:26:13,340 --> 00:26:15,695
all of them are tropical rainforests

486
00:26:15,695 --> 00:26:16,547
rainforest zones

487
00:26:16,547 --> 00:26:18,780
and all of them are burning very severely.

488
00:26:18,780 --> 00:26:21,780
We've already spoken about the Siberian fire.

489
00:26:21,780 --> 00:26:23,780
But what about the Amazon rainforest?

490
00:26:23,780 --> 00:26:28,308
In fact, many of the Amazon fires are deliberately set by people.

491
00:26:29,420 --> 00:26:33,367
Some of them are for felling the trees, to take the timber.

492
00:26:33,760 --> 00:26:37,600
But more often, they burn down the forest afterward in order to raise cattle.

493
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:38,620
And why is that?

494
00:26:38,620 --> 00:26:42,980
Because the market price of cattle, the value of the cattle, is high.

495
00:26:42,980 --> 00:26:45,120
They can be sold off for a good deal more money.

496
00:26:45,120 --> 00:26:48,438
So you'll notice that one of the themes at the climate summit

497
00:26:48,438 --> 00:26:51,119
was to offer subsidies to certain countries

498
00:26:51,119 --> 00:26:53,028
countries such as Brazil, for example

499
00:26:53,028 --> 00:26:56,800
so that it would ban people from clearing the Amazon rainforest.

500
00:26:56,800 --> 00:26:57,567
But the truth is

501
00:26:57,567 --> 00:27:00,556
I'm not sure whether by now it might already be a little too late.

502
00:27:00,556 --> 00:27:01,202
And why is that?

503
00:27:01,202 --> 00:27:03,347
In recent years many studies

504
00:27:03,347 --> 00:27:06,119
have shown the Amazon to be the world's very largest

505
00:27:06,119 --> 00:27:08,107
tropical rainforest zone

506
00:27:08,107 --> 00:27:10,880
you could even call it the earth's first lung.

507
00:27:10,880 --> 00:27:11,925
But here's the thing

508
00:27:11,925 --> 00:27:13,260
some studies show

509
00:27:13,260 --> 00:27:14,827
that the Amazon's carbon emissions

510
00:27:14,827 --> 00:27:17,788
have now surpassed its carbon absorption.

511
00:27:20,340 --> 00:27:21,394
Generally speaking

512
00:27:21,394 --> 00:27:32,720
a rainforest, when there's sunlight and photosynthesis, draws in the carbon dioxide from the atmosphere and turns it into the carbon of its trees; and that is what we call carbon absorption.

513
00:27:32,720 --> 00:27:43,018
But why are the emissions now greater? Well, for one thing, when it catches fire, doesn't it release enormous amounts of carbon dioxide? And it also emits a great many carbon particulates.

514
00:27:43,018 --> 00:27:52,140
The trend over these last few years is that the Amazon's carbon emissions have already exceeded its carbon absorption.

515
00:27:52,140 --> 00:28:00,093
And it makes me think, these days, isn't there always this coronavirus around? And the problem it causes is pneumonia.

516
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:06,140
Pneumonia means you can't breathe; it's as if the earth itself has come down with pneumonia.

517
00:28:06,140 --> 00:28:13,106
Then there's also the Congo Basin in Africa that I mentioned earlier.

518
00:28:13,140 --> 00:28:15,087
This one you could call the world's second lung

519
00:28:15,087 --> 00:28:18,140
and it, too, is a tropical rainforest zone.

520
00:28:18,140 --> 00:28:19,896
In the two photos over on your right

521
00:28:19,896 --> 00:28:24,140
you can see that the firefighting equipment there is really quite primitive.

522
00:28:24,140 --> 00:28:26,321
How do you even fight a great fire like this?

523
00:28:26,321 --> 00:28:28,582
Just with those things, spraying a little here, a little there.

524
00:28:28,582 --> 00:28:31,247
Like a water pistol, how could that ever put it out?

525
00:28:31,247 --> 00:28:33,549
But how did these fires actually start?

526
00:28:33,549 --> 00:28:35,851
In fact, they were set deliberately by people.

527
00:28:35,851 --> 00:28:37,142
Because over in Africa

528
00:28:37,142 --> 00:28:38,939
in order to get food, you have to farm

529
00:28:38,939 --> 00:28:41,060
and how do they go about farming?

530
00:28:41,060 --> 00:28:44,120
They use a method that's called "slash-and-burn."

531
00:28:44,120 --> 00:28:46,180
That is to say, they burn down a patch of forest

532
00:28:46,180 --> 00:28:48,360
and that gives them a clearing to work with.

533
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:51,793
The dead leaves even act like a fertiliser to help the crops along.

534
00:28:51,793 --> 00:28:53,731
And then they plant their crops right there.

535
00:28:53,731 --> 00:28:55,387
And after they've farmed it for a few years

536
00:28:55,387 --> 00:28:58,254
that ground becomes overrun with a great many weeds

537
00:28:58,254 --> 00:29:01,848
so the people move off elsewhere, and burn down yet another patch.

538
00:29:01,848 --> 00:29:03,307
And the plot they'd farmed first

539
00:29:03,307 --> 00:29:04,059
is simply left

540
00:29:04,059 --> 00:29:07,420
to slowly recover, and grow all its plants back again.

541
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:10,740
This is actually how it has always worked, all along.

542
00:29:10,740 --> 00:29:12,740
This slash-and-burn method of working

543
00:29:12,740 --> 00:29:15,100
is called "slash-and-burn cultivation."

544
00:29:15,100 --> 00:29:18,209
So why has it now spiralled so completely out of control?

545
00:29:18,209 --> 00:29:20,592
This actually leads us on to another problem.

546
00:29:20,592 --> 00:29:22,005
Because there are more people now.

547
00:29:22,005 --> 00:29:24,179
Because the population has grown so much

548
00:29:24,179 --> 00:29:25,777
those abandoned plots have no time

549
00:29:25,777 --> 00:29:27,640
not enough time left to recover.

550
00:29:27,640 --> 00:29:30,110
Because there are so many more mouths to feed

551
00:29:30,110 --> 00:29:32,095
they have no choice but to keep on burning

552
00:29:32,095 --> 00:29:35,580
and so the forests are given no time at all to recover.

553
00:29:35,580 --> 00:29:40,180
And so the world's second lung has come down with pneumonia as well.

554
00:29:40,180 --> 00:29:42,500
And this brings us on to the second problem.

555
00:29:42,500 --> 00:29:44,761
And it's that over these last hundred years or so

556
00:29:44,761 --> 00:29:48,355
the world's population has grown at a breakneck speed.

557
00:29:48,355 --> 00:29:50,387
As you can see here from this chart

558
00:29:50,387 --> 00:29:53,754
in 1930 the whole world had 2 billion people.

559
00:29:54,020 --> 00:29:56,503
But by this year, and it's already late November now

560
00:29:56,503 --> 00:29:59,640
the global population has reached 7.9 billion.

561
00:29:59,640 --> 00:30:02,717
According to the projections, in spite of the pandemic

562
00:30:02,717 --> 00:30:05,039
by the end of next year, perhaps

563
00:30:05,039 --> 00:30:09,450
the world's population will break right past 8 billion.

564
00:30:09,620 --> 00:30:11,807
That is to say, going from 2 billion

565
00:30:11,807 --> 00:30:12,977
all the way to 8 billion

566
00:30:12,977 --> 00:30:15,520
in less than a hundred years, all told.

567
00:30:15,520 --> 00:30:18,306
This is a very serious problem indeed.

568
00:30:18,580 --> 00:30:20,811
Because when the population grows so large

569
00:30:20,811 --> 00:30:22,940
the earth's resources are limited.

570
00:30:22,940 --> 00:30:26,897
And which regions are seeing the population grow most explosively?

571
00:30:26,897 --> 00:30:27,800
It's in Africa.

572
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:30,047
So some people have even estimated

573
00:30:30,047 --> 00:30:31,451
that within a few years

574
00:30:31,451 --> 00:30:40,100
the world's largest city will not be New York, nor will it be Tokyo, but a place in Nigeria called Lagos.

575
00:30:40,100 --> 00:30:47,260
Africa is where the population is growing the fastest, and then there's the Middle East region too.

576
00:30:47,260 --> 00:30:54,180
And there's India as well, of course; and China's 1.3 billion are actually ageing very rapidly indeed.

577
00:30:54,180 --> 00:31:02,180
That's why in recent years they've relaxed the one-child policy, encouraging, even urging people to have two or three children.

578
00:31:02,180 --> 00:31:06,180
When there are so very many people, some things simply can't be helped.

579
00:31:06,180 --> 00:31:10,180
So you'll notice that the climate summit didn't even touch upon this issue.

580
00:31:10,180 --> 00:31:18,478
Instead, it leaned entirely on the carbon-emitting nations; the biggest emitter of all is China, and the second is India

581
00:31:18,478 --> 00:31:21,180
both of them countries with well over a billion people.

582
00:31:21,180 --> 00:31:25,011
But you know full well that these promises are only words on paper.

583
00:31:25,380 --> 00:31:28,701
Why is that? Because just last month, for instance

584
00:31:28,701 --> 00:31:33,220
you saw China facing a power shortage up in the northeast, didn't you?

585
00:31:33,220 --> 00:31:36,531
Winter comes around once again, and once again there's no electricity.

586
00:31:36,531 --> 00:31:40,166
And no one even knows where that heating is supposed to come from, or how.

587
00:31:40,166 --> 00:31:44,002
And in China, the power is very often generated by burning coal.

588
00:31:44,002 --> 00:31:48,040
The population is enormous, and there's a power crunch on top of it.

589
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:51,231
Not to mention that all of that manufacturing needs electricity too.

590
00:31:51,231 --> 00:31:53,654
And the power comes mainly from the burning of coal.

591
00:31:53,654 --> 00:31:56,763
So how can it possibly promise to cut its emissions?

592
00:31:56,763 --> 00:31:57,369
So really

593
00:31:57,369 --> 00:32:00,801
this is a very big problem indeed; and in the picture on the right

594
00:32:00,801 --> 00:32:05,364
many people are now concerned with what's called the earth's carrying capacity.

595
00:32:05,364 --> 00:32:07,464
That is to say, just how many people can the earth

596
00:32:07,464 --> 00:32:11,220
with its land area, and its resources, actually manage to support?

597
00:32:11,220 --> 00:32:13,764
Over these past few decades many people have studied this

598
00:32:13,764 --> 00:32:17,923
and most scientists share one consensus, which is this: that the world's

599
00:32:17,923 --> 00:32:20,629
resources can supply around 8 billion people.

600
00:32:20,629 --> 00:32:24,385
But don't forget, with all of these natural disasters and this shifting climate

601
00:32:24,385 --> 00:32:26,283
and I haven't even mentioned drought today

602
00:32:26,283 --> 00:32:28,948
you can imagine that the earth is really a kind of balance.

603
00:32:28,948 --> 00:32:31,819
When some places are being drenched with such heavy rain

604
00:32:31,819 --> 00:32:35,980
then you can well imagine that other places are suffering the most extreme drought.

605
00:32:35,980 --> 00:32:37,676
California is one such example.

606
00:32:37,676 --> 00:32:42,920
And these disasters, at the very same time, lower the earth's carrying capacity.

607
00:32:42,920 --> 00:32:47,180
So really, this is a problem that no one even knows how to solve.

608
00:32:47,180 --> 00:33:07,696
And so, speaking of all this, I'm reminded of a certain ship, the Titanic; the Titanic sank because it struck an iceberg. The story goes that at the moment it hit the iceberg, the captain immediately called a meeting, and because the ship's designer happened to be aboard, and the owner too, they all gathered together

609
00:33:07,696 --> 00:33:19,380
and after inspecting it, they discovered that at the bow, the first, the second, the third, the fourth, and the fifth compartment, that is, the ship's holds, were all taking on water.

610
00:33:19,380 --> 00:33:21,346
And that designer grew very anxious indeed

611
00:33:21,346 --> 00:33:23,480
because he was the one who had designed the ship.

612
00:33:23,480 --> 00:33:24,571
So the captain asked

613
00:33:24,571 --> 00:33:28,080
this water coming in, can it be pumped back out with the pumps?

614
00:33:28,080 --> 00:33:32,037
Everyone looked at one another, and they already knew exactly what was about to happen.

615
00:33:32,037 --> 00:33:34,592
Pumping out the water could only buy them time

616
00:33:34,592 --> 00:33:36,159
just a few minutes, that's all.

617
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:37,088
And why was that?

618
00:33:37,088 --> 00:33:37,779
Because this ship

619
00:33:37,779 --> 00:33:40,829
with three compartments, or four, flooded, could still stay afloat

620
00:33:40,829 --> 00:33:43,960
but with five compartments gone, there was no way it could stay up.

621
00:33:43,960 --> 00:33:45,781
So the ship's owner asked

622
00:33:45,781 --> 00:33:48,320
the owner said, how can this possibly be?

623
00:33:48,320 --> 00:33:50,904
This is her maiden voyage, her very first one.

624
00:33:50,904 --> 00:33:52,633
And that designer answered him

625
00:33:52,633 --> 00:33:54,006
this ship is made of steel

626
00:33:54,006 --> 00:33:55,480
and she will sink.

627
00:33:55,480 --> 00:33:57,628
It's just a mathematical certainty

628
00:33:57,628 --> 00:34:02,330
that is to say, it is a mathematical inevitability.

629
00:34:02,660 --> 00:34:04,767
And when you look at everything we've just seen

630
00:34:04,767 --> 00:34:07,000
it really is a very hard thing to face.

631
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:16,253
It reminds me of a few years back, when Al Gore, after he'd left office as the US Vice President, turned his attention to global climate change

632
00:34:16,253 --> 00:34:20,240
and made a film, called An Inconvenient Truth.

633
00:34:20,240 --> 00:34:24,117
What was the Hong Kong Chinese title again? "The Truth of Despair."

634
00:34:24,117 --> 00:34:27,600
And I feel that, up to this point, there really is no hope at all.

635
00:34:27,600 --> 00:34:32,760
Perhaps you'll say, the whole world can join hands and cut its carbon emissions.

636
00:34:32,760 --> 00:34:36,641
But as I mentioned earlier, at the very least it overlooks one enormous problem

637
00:34:36,641 --> 00:34:38,960
and that is the problem of population.

638
00:34:38,960 --> 00:34:44,169
And getting the nations to take turns cutting emissions together is itself another problem.

639
00:34:44,169 --> 00:34:47,276
So this is exactly why seventy percent of young people

640
00:34:47,276 --> 00:34:49,229
have so little hope for what lies ahead

641
00:34:49,229 --> 00:34:50,960
and are utterly filled with fear.

642
00:34:50,960 --> 00:34:52,715
And thinking it all over, I feel

643
00:34:52,715 --> 00:34:54,552
that if there really were no hope at all

644
00:34:54,552 --> 00:34:56,960
I honestly wouldn't know what on earth to share with you.

645
00:34:56,960 --> 00:34:58,960
But here is something precious.

646
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:01,687
At its very end, the Bible leaves us with this one saying

647
00:35:01,687 --> 00:35:03,960
and it's spoken by the Lord Jesus Himself.

648
00:35:03,960 --> 00:35:05,656
The Lord Jesus, He sent His angel

649
00:35:05,656 --> 00:35:08,725
to testify these things to the churches, and what did He say?

650
00:35:08,725 --> 00:35:11,350
There's a line that follows; what is it that He says?

651
00:35:11,350 --> 00:35:13,369
He says: I am the bright morning star.

652
00:35:13,369 --> 00:35:16,963
You see, the deeper the night grows, the darker and the colder it becomes.

653
00:35:16,963 --> 00:35:20,388
And at the coldest hour of all, just before the dawn

654
00:35:20,388 --> 00:35:22,478
the morning star appears.

655
00:35:22,960 --> 00:35:26,960
And when people see the morning star, they know the dawn is very near.

656
00:35:26,960 --> 00:35:28,818
So what does the Lord mean by this?

657
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:31,514
He means to give people hope.

658
00:35:31,960 --> 00:35:33,121
That when you see Him

659
00:35:33,121 --> 00:35:34,979
the dawn is close at hand.

660
00:35:36,780 --> 00:35:37,800
And why is that?

661
00:35:37,800 --> 00:35:41,380
Because the Lord Jesus has already promised to prepare a place for us.

662
00:35:41,380 --> 00:35:47,520
And once He has prepared that place, He will come again to receive all who believe unto Himself.

663
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:49,229
So the state of this whole world

664
00:35:49,229 --> 00:35:52,317
is really meant to prepare people's hearts

665
00:35:52,317 --> 00:35:53,254
to let people know

666
00:35:53,254 --> 00:35:55,460
that in the world there is no hope.

667
00:35:55,460 --> 00:35:56,885
But how precious it is

668
00:35:56,885 --> 00:35:58,361
that those who have the Lord Jesus

669
00:35:58,361 --> 00:36:01,720
they have a living hope, which is that the Lord Jesus is coming back

670
00:36:01,720 --> 00:36:03,960
to receive us unto Himself.

671
00:36:03,960 --> 00:36:07,037
And now, I'd like to share a hymn with you all.

672
00:36:08,560 --> 00:36:11,669
This hymn is called "The Lord, Like the Bright Morning Star."

673
00:36:11,669 --> 00:36:14,052
It brings to us hope, it brings to us light

674
00:36:14,052 --> 00:36:18,292
so that even in our despair we can still see the way out, far in the distance.

